---
/**
 * Full-screen intro splash: the icon mark draws on first, then the
 * "inking" strokes draw on one at a time like a pen writing them,
 * "storeys" then pops in letter by letter, the whole mark holds, then
 * the icon flies from its splash position to exactly where its
 * counterpart already sits in the fixed Nav header (which has been
 * rendered underneath, hidden, this whole time) - Nav is icon-only, so
 * the wordmark just fades out rather than flying anywhere - while the
 * terracotta backdrop dissolves out from behind them. Reads as the icon
 * relocating into the navbar rather than two unrelated elements
 * swapping places. Shown once per browser session (sessionStorage-
 * gated) - repeat page loads within the same tab skip straight past it.
 *
 * The animation itself is pure CSS (`@keyframes` below), not JS. That's
 * a deliberate choice: an earlier version drove this with a GSAP
 * timeline, which meant a failed/slow GSAP import could leave this
 * full-viewport opaque panel stuck on screen forever with no way to
 * dismiss it - the worst possible failure mode for something in the
 * critical path of every page load. CSS animations start as soon as
 * the browser paints the element, with no JS dependency to fail. JS
 * here is only a *progressive enhancement* layer on top: it decides
 * whether to skip the animation at all (repeat visit, reduced-motion),
 * measures each "inking" stroke's path length (there's no CSS-only way
 * to do this - see the script below) and the icon/wordmark's fly-to-nav
 * distance (ditto - it depends on the Nav's actual rendered position,
 * which varies by viewport size), locks page scroll while it plays,
 * and removes the (by then harmless, invisible) DOM node afterward.
 *
 * The SVG markup below is inlined rather than referenced via <img
 * src="/wordmark-animated.svg">, because the per-stroke/per-letter
 * animations need to select individual <path>/<use> elements from
 * outside the SVG - an <img> is opaque to CSS, so this had to become a
 * duplicate-but-editable copy of that file's content rather than a
 * reference to it. Where each path came from: source is the studio's
 * Illustrator master (converted via `pdftocairo -svg`, since it's a
 * PDF-compatible file), which - unlike the flattened/traced kit export
 * originally used for `/wordmark.svg` - keeps "storeys" as real
 * per-letter glyph references and "inking" as separate un-fused
 * vector paths per pen-stroke (dot, i, n, k, i+n, dot, g - the second
 * "i"+"n" were written as one continuous stroke without lifting the
 * pen, unlike the first). That separability is *why* this animation is
 * possible at all: the flattened kit SVGs are each a single fused
 * compound path with no per-letter or per-stroke boundaries to animate
 * independently.
 *
 * The icon mark draws on too, reusing `/icon.svg`'s existing path (the
 * one already used in Nav.astro) rather than anything from the
 * Illustrator file - the icon in that file turned out to be an
 * embedded raster scan, not vector art, so there was nothing to trace
 * a stroke through. `/icon.svg` works for this anyway because, unlike
 * the flattened wordmark, it was already effectively drawn as one
 * continuous line (a ring, then the "S" body) rather than a filled
 * silhouette - the two subpaths in that one <path> draw in that order
 * simply because stroke-dasharray/dashoffset reveals a path in DOM
 * order, no splitting needed.
 */
---

